## Finding 7 — 🟠 No Input Validation Framework (OWASP A03)

**Location:** Globally — no `express.json()` body parser or validation middleware.

The app currently has no POST/PUT routes, but has no guardrails to prevent adding them unsafely. As the app grows, unvalidated inputs are the primary vector for injection attacks (SQLi, NoSQLi, command injection, path traversal). Establishing a validation framework *now* is far cheaper than retrofitting it later.

**Fix — add body parsing + a validation library from the start:**
```js
const { body, param, query, validationResult } = require('express-validator');

// Parse JSON bodies (required for POST/PUT)
app.use(express.json({ limit: '10kb' })); // Limit body size to prevent payload attacks
app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: false, limit: '10kb' }));

// Reusable validation error handler
function validateRequest(req, res, next) {
  const errors = validationResult(req);
  if (!errors.isEmpty()) {
    return res.status(400).json({ errors: errors.array() });
  }
  next();
}

// Example: validated POST /users route
app.post('/users',
  requireAuth,
  [
    body('name')
      .trim()
      .isLength({ min: 1, max: 100 })
      .escape()                          // Strips HTML — prevents stored XSS
      .withMessage('Name must be 1–100 characters'),
    body('email')
      .isEmail()
      .normalizeEmail()
      .withMessage('Must be a valid email address'),
  ],
  validateRequest,
  (req, res) => {
    // At this point, req.body is safe and validated
    const { name, email } = req.body;
    // ... create user
  }
);
```

