## Finding 8 — 🟡 Hardcoded Data (OWASP A03 / Scalability Risk)

**Location:** `app.js` lines 13–16.

```js
res.json([
  { id: 1, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' },
  { id: 2, name: 'Bob', email: 'bob@example.com' },
]);
```

Hardcoded data means real user data will eventually be moved here or into a string-concatenated query. When a database is introduced, any string-building approach creates **SQL/NoSQL injection** risk. Establishing the parameterized query pattern now avoids this.

**Fix — use parameterized queries from day one:**
```js
// ✅ SAFE — parameterized query (works with pg, mysql2, sqlite3, etc.)
app.get('/users/:id', requireAuth, async (req, res) => {
  const { id } = req.params;

  // Validate the param before it touches the DB
  if (!/^\d+$/.test(id)) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Invalid user ID' });
  }

  // The DB driver escapes `id` — it can NEVER become part of the SQL syntax
  const result = await db.query('SELECT id, name, email FROM users WHERE id = $1', [id]);

  if (result.rows.length === 0) {
    return res.status(404).json({ error: 'User not found' });
  }
  res.json(result.rows[0]);
});

// ❌ NEVER DO THIS — string concatenation = SQL injection
// db.query(`SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ${req.params.id}`)
```

