## Finding 8 — 🟠 No Request Body Parsing Limits (OWASP A05)

**Problem:** There is no `express.json()` middleware at all. Once it's added (as it almost certainly will be), the default body size limit is `100kb`, but it's easy to misconfigure. Without explicit limits, large payloads can be used to exhaust memory.

**Fix:** Add body parsing middleware with an explicit, conservative limit.

```js
// BAD — default or no limit
app.use(express.json());

// GOOD — explicit, restrictive limit
app.use(express.json({ limit: '10kb' }));
app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: true, limit: '10kb' }));
```

---

## Finding 9 — 🟡 No Error Handling / Verbose Leak Risk (OWASP A09)

**Problem:** There is no global error handler. Without one, Express falls back to its default behavior, which can leak stack traces, internal paths, and dependency versions to the client in development mode — and silently swallow errors in production.

**Fix:** Add a structured, environment-aware global error handler.

```js
// Place this AFTER all route definitions

// 404 handler — catches requests to undefined routes
app.use((req, res) => {
  res.status(404).json({ error: 'Not found' });
});

// Global error handler — catches all errors passed via next(err)
app.use((err, req, res, next) => { // eslint-disable-line no-unused-vars
  const statusCode = err.status || err.statusCode || 500;

  // Log internally with full detail
  console.error({
    message: err.message,
    stack: err.stack,
    path: req.path,
    method: req.method,
    ip: req.ip,
    timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
  });

  // Respond externally with minimal detail
  const response = {
    error: statusCode < 500 ? err.message : 'Internal server error',
