## Finding 7 — 🟡 No Global Error Handler (Stack Trace Leakage Risk)
**OWASP A05:2021 – Security Misconfiguration**

Express's default error handler will send full stack traces to the client in development and can expose internal paths, dependency versions, and logic in production if `NODE_ENV` is not set correctly. There is also no handler for unhandled promise rejections.

**Fix:**
```js
// ✅ Catch-all for 404s (must come after all routes)
app.use((req, res) => {
  res.status(404).json({ error: 'Not found' });
});

// ✅ Global error handler (must have 4 args for Express to recognize it)
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {  // eslint-disable-line no-unused-vars
  const isProd = process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production';

  // Log full error internally...
  logger.error('Unhandled error', {
    message: err.message,
    stack:   err.stack,
    path:    req.path,
    method:  req.method,
  });

  // ...but never expose internals to the client
  res.status(err.status || 500).json({
    error: isProd ? 'An unexpected error occurred.' : err.message,
  });
});

// ✅ Handle async/promise failures that bypass Express
process.on('unhandledRejection', (reason) => {
  logger.error('Unhandled Promise Rejection', { reason });
});

process.on('uncaughtException', (err) => {
  logger.error('Uncaught Exception — shutting down', { err });
  process.exit(1);   // Crash intentionally; let a process manager (pm2/k8s) restart
});
```

