### Tier 2 — Production (use a real secrets manager)

For any real deployment, inject secrets from a secrets manager rather than files. All major platforms have a native option:

| Platform | Recommended Approach |
|---|---|
| AWS | Secrets Manager or Parameter Store via IAM role |
| GCP | Secret Manager via Workload Identity |
| Azure | Key Vault via Managed Identity |
| Kubernetes | Sealed Secrets or External Secrets Operator |
| Any | HashiCorp Vault |

The integration pattern for AWS looks like this:
```js
// secrets.js  ← replaces .env in production
const { SecretsManagerClient, GetSecretValueCommand } = require('@aws-sdk/client-secrets-manager');

async function loadSecrets() {
  // Only use secrets manager outside of local dev
  if (process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production') {
    require('dotenv').config();
    return;
  }

  const client = new SecretsManagerClient({ region: process.env.AWS_REGION });
  const response = await client.send(
    new GetSecretValueCommand({ SecretId: 'myapp/production' })
  );
  const secrets = JSON.parse(response.SecretString);

  // Inject into process.env so the rest of the app is unaware of the source
  Object.assign(process.env, secrets);
}

module.exports = { loadSecrets };
```

```js
// app.js — load secrets before anything else
const { loadSecrets } = require('./secrets');

(async () => {
  await loadSecrets();          // must resolve before routes are registered
  const config = require('./config');
  const app    = require('./server'); // the express setup, moved to server.js
  app.listen(config.port);
})();
```

