## 🧱 Recommended File Structure

```
project/
├── .env              ← local only, gitignored
├── .env.example      ← committed, no real values
├── .gitignore        ← must list .env
├── config.js         ← single source of truth for all config
├── secrets.js        ← environment-aware secret loading
├── app.js            ← express setup only, no process.env calls
└── server.js         ← entry point, bootstraps secrets then starts app
```

The core principle: **`process.env` should only ever be read in `config.js`**. Every other file imports from `config`. This means if you ever migrate from `.env` → Vault → AWS Secrets Manager, you change exactly one file.

